    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe behavioural redesign is mapped onto the code well: `Soundex` is a clean, single-responsibility class placed in `model` beside `Owner`/`OwnerIdentity`/`OwnerTelephones`, which is exactly where this codebase keeps shared derivation helpers; `AssignPossibleDuplicate` and `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` correctly delegate to `OwnerIdentity.of`/`Soundex.of` rather than reimplementing the key; the household-duplicate block is gone as the spec requires; and every javadoc across the touched files was updated accurately. One structural issue.\n\n**Finding \u2014 identical `sha256Hex` helper duplicated (`OwnerIdentity.java:38-53`)**\n\nThe change adds a private `sha256Hex(String)` to `OwnerIdentity` that is byte-for-byte identical to the existing `OwnerHousehold.sha256Hex` (`OwnerHousehold.java:79-93`) \u2014 same lowercase `Character.forDigit` hex encoding, same `NoSuchAlgorithmException`\u2192`IllegalStateException` wrapping. Before this change `OwnerIdentity` did no hashing, so this diff is what *introduces* the duplication, and it does so within the same owner-identity/household derivation family.\n\nThis is the exact case for extracting instead of copying, and the codebase's own convention points to the clean home: shared derivation primitives (`OwnerTelephones`, `OwnerIdentity`, and now `Soundex`) live in `model`. A public `model` helper (e.g. `Hashing.sha256Hex`) would let `OwnerIdentity` and `OwnerHousehold` (which can import from `model`) share one copy, matching the same \"single definition lives in `model`\" pattern the author already followed for `Soundex`.\n\nSuggested: add the hex-SHA-256 primitive to `model` as a small shared helper and have both `OwnerIdentity` and `OwnerHousehold` call it, removing the duplicated private method.\n\n(Note: `AssignCustomerCode.hash8` is a *different* helper \u2014 uppercase, truncated to 8 chars via `String.format` \u2014 so it's a deliberate variant, not part of this duplication, though it could optionally reuse the same primitive.)",
